---
import BaseLayout from '../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro'
import ArchDocsNav from '../../../components/docs/ArchDocsNav.astro'

const GH = 'https://github.com/tamatebako'

const repos = [
  {
    name: 'dwarfs-t',
    href: `${GH}/dwarfs-t`,
    role: 'Thrift-free DwarFS fork — the high-compression read-only filesystem at the bottom of the stack, plus the dwarfs_c_* C ABI (writer included) that Rust binds.',
    state: 'Active',
    fate: 'C++ forever by decision (it tracks upstream DwarFS format evolution). Never archived — the foundation, not a legacy repo.',
    status: 'shipped',
  },
  {
    name: 'dwarfs-t-rs',
    href: `${GH}/dwarfs-t-rs`,
    role: 'Standalone Rust binding to dwarfs-t: the safe dwarfs-t crate (reader + in-process Writer) over dwarfs-t-sys\u2019s hand-pinned FFI. Consumed by tebako-rs as an ordinary external dependency.',
    state: 'Active',
    fate: 'Active forever — general-purpose, crates.io-bound (tags + cargo publish + docs.rs when it publishes). Never inside tebako-rs.',
    status: 'shipped',
  },
  {
    name: 'libtfs',
    href: `${GH}/libtfs`,
    role: 'The C++ VFS engine: tebako_fs_* C ABI (24 exports, multi-mount), dwarfs/squashfs/zip backends, mkdwarfs + tebakofs binaries. Currently v0.13.0 — the parity oracle for the Rust tfs crate.',
    state: 'Active (oracle)',
    fate: 'Maintenance (security/critical fixes only) once tebako-rs ships tebako_fs_* parity RELEASES AND tebako-runtime-ruby consumes them — the tfs crate is at parity in the workspace; the releases are the pending step. Archival gate below.',
    status: 'shipped',
  },
  {
    name: 'tebako-rs',
    href: `${GH}/tebako-rs`,
    role: 'The Rust workspace: tpkg, tfs, tebako-pkg, tfs-cli, tebako-cli, tebako-bootstrap, tebako-http, tebako-signer, sqfs-sys — all shipped and oracle-verified. No downloadable releases yet.',
    state: 'Active',
    fate: 'The future home of the CLI, the ABI implementation, the bootstrap, and the shim. Release pipeline (signed per-platform binaries) is the open item.',
    status: 'shipped',
  },
  {
    name: 'ruby',
    href: `${GH}/ruby`,
    role: 'Canonical patches + versions.yml + the patched-source pipeline (release monitor, lint matrix, release-src). Currently v0.2.1 — 76 scenario assets.',
    state: 'Active',
    fate: 'Never archived — build/metadata tooling, language-correct as-is (not C++).',
    status: 'shipped',
  },
  {
    name: 'tebako-runtime-ruby',
    href: `${GH}/tebako-runtime-ruby`,
    role: 'The runtime factory: builds patched source per ruby × platform, publishes runtime packages + manifest.json. Currently v0.15.9 — 114 packages; dual-publish of .tfs runtime images alongside the executables is rolling out.',
    state: 'Active',
    fate: 'Never archived — build/metadata tooling (not C++).',
    status: 'shipped',
  },
  {
    name: 'tebako',
    href: `${GH}/tebako`,
    role: 'The gem: press (lean/fat/classic), scenario + Gemfile handling, runtime/bootstrap resolution, cache CLI. The reference implementation tebako-cli was ported against (golden byte-parity).',
    state: 'Maintenance-bound',
    fate: 'Retires at 0.15.x with a deprecation note once tebako-cli ships releases (owner decision 2026-07-26: tebako-cli IS the product; a pure-Ruby installer shim may preserve the gem-install habit — an FFI wrapper was evaluated and rejected). Archival gate below.',
    status: 'shipped',
  },
  {
    name: 'tebako-bootstrap',
    href: `${GH}/tebako-bootstrap`,
    role: 'The C99 launcher (part A of the three-part package), v0.2.0 — six platform binaries, 32–908 KB. Now the behavioral oracle for the Rust tebako-bootstrap crate.',
    state: 'Maintenance',
    fate: 'Stays the released default until the tebako-rs release pipeline ships; archived only when the Rust bootstrap ships and is consumed as the bootstrap slot.',
    status: 'shipped',
  },
  {
    name: 'tebako-ci-containers',
    href: `${GH}/tebako-ci-containers`,
    role: 'Container images the runtime matrix builds in.',
    state: 'Active',
    fate: 'Never archived.',
    status: 'shipped',
  },
  {
    name: 'tebako-samples',
    href: `${GH}/tebako-samples`,
    role: 'Sample applications and packaging tutorials.',
    state: 'Active',
    fate: 'Never archived.',
    status: 'shipped',
  },
  {
    name: 'tebako.org',
    href: `${GH}/tebako.org`,
    role: 'This website.',
    state: 'Active',
    fate: 'Never archived.',
    status: 'shipped',
  },
  {
    name: 'fmem',
    href: `${GH}/fmem`,
    role: 'Cross-platform memory-backed libc streams — a dependency of the legacy stack.',
    state: 'Orphaned',
    fate: 'The archival candidate: no longer consumed by any repo in the modern stack (libtfs mounts images from memory directly). Still not archived on GitHub as of this writing — it goes when the owners pull the trigger, not before.',
    status: 'shipped',
  },
]

const gates = [
  {
    repo: 'libtfs (C++)',
    href: `${GH}/libtfs`,
    gate: 'libtfs-rs parity sustained for 2 consecutive releases + tebako-runtime-ruby pinned to tebako-rs + the gem/CLI fully off C-libtfs. Progress: the tfs crate is at parity in the workspace — the releases do not exist yet.',
  },
  {
    repo: 'tebako (gem)',
    href: `${GH}/tebako`,
    gate: 'tebako-cli press parity (lean/fat/classic) green end-to-end on 3 platforms + one full release cycle of dual availability. Progress: golden byte-parity for lean/fat is proven in CI; classic mode and the tebako-rs releases are pending.',
  },
  {
    repo: 'tebako-bootstrap',
    href: `${GH}/tebako-bootstrap`,
    gate: 'Only if a Rust bootstrap ships and is consumed by tebako-pkg/tebako-cli as the bootstrap slot. Progress: the Rust bootstrap is behavior-complete and size-gated in the workspace; tebako-cli already defaults to it in-workspace — consumption via released binaries is the pending step.',
  },
]
---

<BaseLayout title="Repository map and lifecycle — Tebako architecture" description="Every tamatebako repository, its role, its shipped state, and its archival gate.">
  <section class="mx-auto max-w-5xl px-6 pb-24 pt-32">
    <p class="tb-eyebrow">ARCHITECTURE · 07</p>
    <h1 class="mt-3">Repository map &amp; lifecycle.</h1>
    <p class="mt-4 max-w-2xl text-lg leading-relaxed text-[var(--tb-c-text-2)]">
      The stack is a fleet of small repos, each owning one artifact and one contract.
      None of them is disposable by default — archival is an end state with explicit
      gates, never a starting move.
    </p>

    <ArchDocsNav />

    <div class="mt-12 overflow-x-auto rounded-2xl border border-[var(--tb-c-divider)]" style="background: var(--tb-c-bg-soft);">
      <table class="w-full min-w-[820px] text-left text-sm">
        <thead>
          <tr class="border-b border-[var(--tb-c-divider-strong)] font-[var(--font-mono)] text-xs text-[var(--tb-c-text-3)]">
            <th class="px-5 py-3 font-medium">REPO</th>
            <th class="px-5 py-3 font-medium">ROLE</th>
            <th class="px-5 py-3 font-medium">STATE</th>
            <th class="px-5 py-3 font-medium">FATE</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody class="divide-y divide-[var(--tb-c-divider)]">
          {repos.map((r) => (
            <tr>
              <td class="px-5 py-3 font-[var(--font-mono)] text-xs whitespace-nowrap">
                <a href={r.href} class="tb-link-underline text-[var(--tb-c-accent)]">{r.name}</a>
              </td>
              <td class="px-5 py-3 text-[var(--tb-c-text-2)]">{r.role}</td>
              <td class="px-5 py-3 whitespace-nowrap">
                <span class="font-[var(--font-mono)] text-xs text-[var(--tb-c-text-2)]">{r.state}</span>
              </td>
              <td class="px-5 py-3 text-[var(--tb-c-text-2)]">{r.fate}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-16">
      <h2 class="mb-3">Archival gates.</h2>
      <p class="mb-8 max-w-2xl text-[var(--tb-c-text-2)]">
        A repo is archived only when its gate is fully met — existing released artifacts
        must remain reproducible until every consumer has migrated off them. Three repos
        carry gates; the rest are either permanent foundations or not C++ at all.
      </p>
      <div class="space-y-4">
        {gates.map((g) => (
          <div class="rounded-2xl border border-[var(--tb-c-divider)] p-6" style="background: var(--tb-c-bg-soft);">
            <p class="font-[var(--font-mono)] text-sm font-medium text-[var(--tb-c-accent)]">{g.repo}</p>
            <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-[var(--tb-c-text-2)]">{g.gate}</p>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-16">
      <h2 class="mb-3">The lifecycle rules.</h2>
      <div class="grid gap-4 md:grid-cols-2">
        <div class="tb-card p-6">
          <h3 class="font-[var(--font-display)] text-lg font-medium text-[var(--tb-c-accent)]">Contracts outlive implementations</h3>
          <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-[var(--tb-c-text-2)]">
            The <code class="font-[var(--font-mono)] text-xs">tebako_fs_*</code> ABI and the tpkg
            trailer are byte-level contracts. Consumers don't care which repo ships them — that's
            what made the Rust reimplementation possible without flag days, and the C++ side now
            serves as the parity oracle for exactly that reason.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="tb-card p-6">
          <h3 class="font-[var(--font-display)] text-lg font-medium text-[var(--tb-c-accent)]">Releases are the interface</h3>
          <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-[var(--tb-c-text-2)]">
            Repos consume each other's published releases, never source trees. The gem and
            the runtime factory live in different repos with separate cadences — the
            manifest.json contract sits between them, so the packager never hardcodes a
            runtime matrix.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="tb-card p-6">
          <h3 class="font-[var(--font-display)] text-lg font-medium text-[var(--tb-c-accent)]">Maintenance mode is a state, not a graveyard</h3>
          <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-[var(--tb-c-text-2)]">
            A maintenance-mode repo still gets security and critical fixes. It stops getting
            features — and it leaves maintenance mode if its replacement slips. The C++
            bootstrap is exactly here: released default, oracle for its Rust successor.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="tb-card p-6">
          <h3 class="font-[var(--font-display)] text-lg font-medium text-[var(--tb-c-accent)]">dwarfs-t is not legacy</h3>
          <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-[var(--tb-c-text-2)]">
            A Rust DwarFS reader/writer is explicitly out of scope. The only Rust-consumable
            surface is the C API inside dwarfs-t itself, co-versioned with the C++ it wraps —
            which is what dwarfs-t-rs binds.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
